            <ArticleTitle>Acalculous Cholecystitis in Burn Patients: Is There a Role for Percutaneous Cholecystostomy?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>567-571</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0094-1298(17)30040-8</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cps.2017.02.025</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although acute acalculous cholecystitis is uncommon in burn patients, this condition can be rapidly fatal due to delays in diagnosis and treatment and should always be considered in the differential diagnosis when burn patients become septic, develop abdominal pain, or have hemodynamic instability. This article reviews the use of percutaneous cholecystostomy in burn patients as both a diagnostic and therapeutic intervention.</AbstractText>
